# G105 — Event Scheduler

*v2. See `event-scheduler.md` for prose: interval-overlap conflict detection,
calendar events as half-open intervals, day-agenda as time-window slice.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Daily Calendar

[daily-calendar @calendar-path
    IN ORDER TO
        render today's agenda
        accept user-proposed events that don't conflict
        and surface conflicts when they do

    @cal <- [calendar[load {path: @calendar-path}]]
    @today-start <- [clock[start-of-day {ms: [clock[now]]}]]
    @today-end   <- @today-start + 86400000

    @today-events <- [cal[events-between {calendar: @cal,
                                            from-ms: @today-start,
                                            to-ms: @today-end}]]
    #agenda @today-events

    sync @user-events {
        propose @title @start-ms @end-ms ->
            @conflicts <- [cal[find-conflicts {calendar: @cal,
                                                 start-ms: @start-ms,
                                                 end-ms: @end-ms}]]
            @conflicts
                where count is zero
                [cal[add {calendar: @cal,
                           event: {title: @title,
                                   start-ms: @start-ms, end-ms: @end-ms}}]]
                || [ui[show-conflicts {ids: @conflicts}]]
    }
]

---

## Verb Surface

[cal
    new
    add                  {calendar, event}
    get                  {calendar, id}                   ;; || not-found
    events-between       {calendar, from-ms, to-ms}
    find-conflicts       {calendar, start-ms, end-ms}
    cancel               {calendar, id}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: half-open intervals as standard

[cal[find-conflicts {calendar: @c, start-ms: @s, end-ms: @e}
    where
        events are half-open intervals [start-ms, end-ms)
        and two intervals conflict iff they overlap (a.end > b.start and b.end > a.start)
        and back-to-back events (a.end == b.start) do not conflict
            so a 9-10am and a 10-11am meeting are adjacent, not overlapping
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    @c <- [cal[new]]
    [cal[add {calendar: @c,
               event: {id: 1, title: "Standup",
                       start-ms: @9am, end-ms: @9_30am}}]]
    [cal[add {calendar: @c,
               event: {id: 2, title: "Lunch",
                       start-ms: @12pm, end-ms: @1pm}}]]

    -> [cal[find-conflicts {calendar: @c,
                             start-ms: @9_15am, end-ms: @9_45am}]]
    ;; -> [1]   (overlaps standup)

    -> [cal[find-conflicts {calendar: @c,
                             start-ms: @9_30am, end-ms: @10am}]]
    ;; -> []    (back-to-back, not overlapping)
]
